GUIDES={
 'compare-hvac-estimates': ('How to compare HVAC estimates.', 'A lower number can hide a different job. Here’s what to put side by side before you choose an installation.', '<h2>Start with the scope.</h2><p>Two prices are only comparable when they describe the same work. Read the equipment model numbers, capacity, controls, removal, disposal, and installation materials. If something is missing, ask whether it is included.</p><h2>Ask who handles the details.</h2><ul><li>Who checks the equipment selection against the house and existing system?</li><li>What changes are included for ductwork, venting, drains, utilities, and controls?</li><li>Who handles permits or additional electrical work, and are those costs included?</li><li>Who starts up and checks the completed system?</li><li>What happens if an unexpected condition changes the price?</li></ul><h2>Read parts and labor separately.</h2><p>Ask for the parts warranty and the contractor’s labor coverage in writing, including registration responsibilities, maintenance requirements, exclusions, and who you call for service.</p><h2>Make the payment schedule clear.</h2><p>Understand the deposit, when remaining payments are due, and any financing or payment processing charges. Ask for the total project cost rather than comparing monthly payments alone.</p><h2>Choose the people as carefully as the equipment.</h2><p>Ask who will install it, how the home will be protected, and what the handoff includes. A clear explanation before the job is a useful sign of how communication will work during it.</p>'),
 'plan-a-tankless-conversion': ('Thinking about tankless?', 'A better conversation starts with your home, your household, and the complete installation.', '<h2>Start with how you use hot water.</h2><p>Tell the contractor about your bathrooms, the fixtures you use at the same time, and what you want to improve. More detail makes for a better assessment than choosing equipment by a model name alone.</p><h2>The installation matters.</h2><p>Ask the contractor to evaluate the existing utilities, proposed location, vent routing, condensate needs, service access, and any required changes. An existing tank installation does not establish that a tankless unit is a direct swap.</p><h2>What should the estimate show?</h2><ul><li>The proposed model and why it fits the household</li><li>Removal and disposal of the old Water Heater</li><li>Included utility, venting, and drainage work</li><li>Permit responsibilities and any additional trade work</li><li>Parts and labor warranty terms</li><li>Maintenance recommendations and service access</li></ul><h2>Ask what ownership looks like.</h2><p>Discuss ongoing maintenance, local water conditions, and what to expect from the system in daily use. Confirm those details with the contractor and the selected equipment’s manufacturer guidance.</p><h2>Get an assessment before you commit.</h2><p>Middletown HVAC handles Water Heater and tankless inquiries through Wazoozie. Share your town, existing equipment, and a short description of your household’s needs.</p>'),
 'prepare-for-service': ('A smoother service visit starts here.', 'A few useful details can help the office send the right person and plan the visit.', '<h2>Explain the problem in your own words.</h2><p>What changed? When did it start? Is the problem constant or intermittent? Does it affect the whole home or one area? You don’t need to diagnose the equipment to make a helpful service request.</p><h2>Gather information without opening equipment.</h2><ul><li>Equipment type and a visible brand or model label</li><li>A short description of what you’ve noticed</li><li>Any recent service reports or approved estimates</li><li>Access instructions, parking, pets, or property-management requirements</li><li>The best person to approve work and receive updates</li></ul><h2>Confirm the visit.</h2><p>Ask about the appointment window, service area, diagnostic charge, and how repair pricing will be approved. For a rental property, agree in advance on who provides access and who authorizes work.</p><h2>Leave space for the technician.</h2><p>Make the route to the equipment accessible where it is safe to do so. Do not open panels or attempt repairs to prepare for a visit.</p><h2>Need an urgent response?</h2><p>Call the business directly to ask about availability. An email draft or online request is not an emergency dispatch.</p>')
}

SERVICES.update({
 'maintenance':dict(label='Maintenance & Comfort Plans',key='hvac',eyebrow='TAKE CARE OF THE COMFORT YOU HAVE.',title='A little care.<br>Season after season.',desc='Explore seasonal HVAC maintenance and Get Comfortable Plans with Middletown HVAC in Delco, Chester County and the Main Line.',intro='Keep the next season on your calendar. Talk with Middletown HVAC about maintenance for the equipment you have and a plan that fits your home.',sections=[('Make maintenance simpler.','<p>Middletown HVAC offers seasonal maintenance and Get Comfortable Plans. Tell the office what equipment you have, how many systems serve the home, and whether you already have a plan.</p><p>Ask which visits are included, what repair discounts apply, how additional systems are handled, and what the current renewal terms are. Confirm pricing and coverage before enrolling.</p>'),('One home. The right kind of visit.','<ul><li>Furnace and Air Conditioner maintenance</li><li>Heat pump and ductless maintenance</li><li>Gas and oil Boiler service inquiries</li><li>Equipment-specific maintenance recommendations</li></ul><p>Tell the office about any current problem when you schedule. A maintenance visit and a repair diagnostic serve different purposes; the team can help choose the right appointment.</p>'),('Already a Middletown customer?','<p>Mention your existing equipment, previous service, and plan status. The office can review your account and confirm whether you need a renewal, an additional system, or a one-time maintenance visit.</p>')],faqs=[('Can I cover more than one system?','Ask the office about adding systems to your plan and confirm the current cost and included visits.'),('Does a plan include every repair?','Ask for the current plan terms, included work, exclusions, and applicable repair discounts. Do not assume a maintenance plan includes all repairs.')]),
 'replacement-estimates':dict(label='HVAC replacement estimates',key='hvac',eyebrow='PLAN THE WORK. KNOW THE PEOPLE.',title='Your next system.<br>Thought through.',desc='Plan a Furnace, Air Conditioner, heat pump or ductless replacement with Middletown HVAC. Clear scope and direct estimate requests.',intro='A replacement should solve the problems you actually have. Start with a conversation about comfort, the house, and what matters to you.',sections=[('Start with the home, not a model number.','<p>Tell Middletown HVAC about rooms that feel different, recurring repairs, equipment age if known, and any changes you’re considering. Explain whether you want to repair an immediate problem or plan a future installation.</p>'),('What to have ready.','<ul><li>Your address and equipment type</li><li>Your comfort concerns and what you want to improve</li><li>Any previous findings or written estimates</li><li>Photos of the equipment and installation space, when available</li><li>Your preferred timing and any access requirements</li></ul>'),('Ask for a complete proposal.','<p>The written proposal should describe the selected equipment, included installation work, removal, controls, warranty terms, payment schedule, and permit responsibilities. Additional electrical work or other trades should be clearly assigned.</p><p><a href="/guides/compare-hvac-estimates/">Read the homeowner’s guide to comparing estimates →</a></p>')],faqs=[('Is this a service call or an estimate?','If equipment has stopped working and the cause is unknown, explain that to the office. They can confirm whether you need a diagnostic visit or a replacement consultation and any applicable charge.'),('Can I discuss heat pumps or ductless options?','Yes. Ask about options suited to the house and installation conditions. Equipment selection follows an assessment of your home and needs.')])
})

SERVICES['second-opinion']=dict(label='HVAC second opinions',key='hvac',eyebrow='A LITTLE CLARITY BEFORE A BIG DECISION.',title='Before you replace it.<br>Take another look.',desc='Ask Middletown HVAC about a second opinion on an HVAC repair or replacement recommendation. Confirm the assessment scope and diagnostic charge.',intro='An expensive recommendation deserves a clear explanation. Talk with Middletown HVAC about the findings you already have and whether an onsite assessment makes sense.',sections=[('Start with what you were told.','<p>Have the other company’s written recommendation, proposed work, and equipment information ready. Explain the problem you called about and what you want a second opinion to clarify.</p><p>An initial conversation or document review is not an onsite diagnosis. Middletown HVAC will confirm what kind of visit is appropriate, the current diagnostic charge, and appointment availability before booking.</p>'),('What to have ready.','<ul><li>Your town and service address</li><li>The equipment type and visible model information</li><li>The existing recommendation or estimate</li><li>What the equipment is doing and when the problem started</li><li>Any relevant service history or photos you already have</li></ul><p>Call first to confirm how the office wants to receive documents or photos. This site does not upload or store them.</p>'),('Make the next decision a clearer one.','<p>The goal is to understand the findings, practical repair options, and the scope of any recommended replacement. A second opinion does not guarantee that an existing recommendation will change.</p><p><a href="/guides/compare-hvac-estimates/">What to compare in a written HVAC proposal →</a></p>')],faqs=[('Is a second opinion free?','Confirm the current charge and scope with the office. Do not assume that an assessment, document review, or service visit is free.'),('Can I upload an estimate here?','Not in this version. Call Middletown HVAC to confirm how to send documents and whether an assessment is appropriate.')])

